<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Register the model servers you use — OpenAI, OpenRouter, Ollama, llama.cpp,
    vLLM, anything that speaks the OpenAI API — and group them into workspaces.
    A client pointed at a workspace sees one <code>/v1</code> that lists every
    model its providers offer and sends each request to the right one. It is
    the model-server counterpart of
    <a href="https://cubicecho.github.io/mcp-router/" rel="noopener">mcp-router</a>.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>One endpoint, every model</h3>
        <p>
          Models are served as <code>&lt;provider&gt;/&lt;model&gt;</code>, so
          <code>ollama/qwen3:32b</code> and <code>openai/gpt-5</code> sit in
          the same list and the id says where a request goes.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Workspaces choose what a client sees</h3>
        <p>
          Each workspace has its own base URL and serves the providers you
          switch on, all of their models or a subset you pick.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A thin pass-through</h3>
        <p>
          Requests are forwarded with only <code>model</code> rewritten, and
          responses, streams included, come back unchanged. Provider keys stay
          on the router and are never shown again.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

